  "body": "**Status:** OPEN. **Owner:** unassigned (to be named by the repo owner). **Branch:** none yet. **Decision needed:** the intended cancellation contract (below).\nFound while reviewing PR #104 (B2c). Separate from #104: #104 fixes router-level resource cleanup; this is a cancellation-semantics defect one layer above it.\n\n## Observed behaviour (verified at `main` 16d663a)\n`Worker.execute()` (`core/workers/base.py:293`) catches `asyncio.CancelledError`, emits `worker.cancelled`, and **returns** `WorkerResult(success=False, error=\"Worker cancelled\")`. The workflow retry loop (`core/workflow/runtime.py:865`) checks only the workflow token (`cancel_token.is_cancelled`), never whether the task was cancelled, so it treats that result as an ordinary node failure and may start another attempt. `WorkflowRuntime.execute()` then returns normally.\n\nProbe (real `WorkflowRuntime` + `ExecutionRuntime` + `Worker.execute` + `ModelRouter`; only the provider stream faked): the task running `execute()` was cancelled at 0.5 s with node retry policy `max_retries=1`. Result: `execute()` returned a result at ~2.5 s, `task.cancelled()` was `False`, and the node ran **twice** (attempts=2). Script: `docs/handoffs/fz02_gate_probe.py` on branch `follow-up/fz02-b2d-latent-token-cancellation-oct2026` (and `docs/handoffs/fz02_b2c/fz02_gate_probe.py` on `wip/fz02-b2c-cancellation-checkpoint-oct2026`); run with `OCROOT=<checkout> python3 <path>`.\n\n## Why this is not fixed by #104\n#104 guarantees provider-stream cleanup when the relevant task is cancelled. It does not make execution stop on cancellation. Successful cleanup must not be read as successful cancellation of the workflow.\n\n## Decision required before any fix (not taken)\nWhat is the cancellation contract for a worker? Options to decide, not a recommendation: (a) `Worker.execute()` re-raises `CancelledError` after emitting its event; (b) it keeps returning a result but marks it as cancelled (distinct from failure) and the workflow retry loop and other callers honour that; (c) a hybrid. Also decide: should a cancelled node ever be retried; what `worker.cancelled` and `WorkerResult` mean downstream; and how this interacts with `ExecutionRuntime.invoke()` and `WorkflowRuntime.execute()` (\"never raises\").\n\n## Required for this item\nIts own owner, its own branch, tests that fail on `main` and pass after (task cancel mid-node must not produce a retry; must not return as an ordinary failure), and an explicit owner decision on the contract recorded before implementation.\n\n## Related cancellation gaps that remain OPEN (not solved by #104)\n- The graph watchdog only sets the workflow token (`core/runtime/watchdog.py:180,196`); it does not cancel node tasks. A node hung in the router ran until the router's own deadline (probe 1, 2.03 s against a 0.3 s graph budget).\n- No production caller threads a cancellation token to the router or `AdapterRuntime` (`planner.py:321-345`, `capability_executor.py:116-121`, `orchestrator.py:923`); the token-driven work is preserved, unreviewed, on `follow-up/fz02-b2d-latent-token-cancellation-oct2026`.\n- Shutdown, Ctrl-C and client-disconnect cancellation are UNVERIFIED.\n- Physical provider-side termination on cancel is UNVERIFIED.\n",
